Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the Next.js project

## Overview
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics by following the latest official documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed Dependencies
- Added `@vercel/analytics` package (v2.0.1)
- Used `--legacy-peer-deps` flag to handle React 19 compatibility (project uses React 19.2.8)

### 2. Modified Files
**Content/app/layout.tsx**
- Added import: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the body tag
- Followed Next.js App Router best practices by placing the component in the root layout

**Content/package.json**
- Added dependency: `"@vercel/analytics": "^2.0.1"`

**Content/package-lock.json**
- Updated with new dependency tree (394 new packages)

## Implementation Details
- Used the framework-specific instructions for Next.js App Router from the official docs
- Placed the Analytics component at the end of the body tag to avoid blocking page rendering
- Preserved all existing code structure and functionality
- Analytics component will automatically track page views once deployed to Vercel

## Verification
✅ Build completed successfully - no errors
✅ Linter passed with no issues
✅ All dependencies installed correctly

## Next Steps for Deployment
1. Deploy to Vercel (if not already deployed)
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics → Enable)
3. After deployment, verify analytics are working by checking browser Network tab for requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`
4. View analytics data in the Vercel dashboard after user visits accumulate

## Notes
- The Analytics component automatically handles development vs production mode
- No additional configuration is required for basic page view tracking
- Custom event tracking is available for Pro/Enterprise plans if needed in the future
